WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.832
Here we create a clean preview view for the customers table.

00:00:03.832 --> 00:00:12.727
The view standardizes the name, cleans the email, creates a ten-digit phone candidate when the number passes the

00:00:12.727 --> 00:00:16.957
selected rule, normalizes blank addresses, and keeps the signup date.

00:00:16.957 --> 00:00:20.009
The original customers table is not changed.

00:00:20.476 --> 00:00:27.143
This is useful while developing cleaning logic because you can compare the clean preview with the source at any time.

00:00:27.143 --> 00:00:31.733
Suppose you later decide that phone numbers should allow an international format.

00:00:31.733 --> 00:00:37.913
You can revise the view logic without trying to reconstruct values that were permanently overwritten.

00:00:37.913 --> 00:00:42.913
Run the view and compare several rows with the original customers table.

00:00:42.913 --> 00:00:49.558
A preview layer gives us a safe place to test analytical transformations before deciding whether any permanent

00:00:49.558 --> 00:00:51.491
correction is justified.
